														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4420815">
									

<p>That's fair &#8212; keep in mind, I'm out of the loop of this stuff (I've never even <em>played</em> a game with lootboxes!), and for me, the really interesting question was less in the video-game parallels (which struck me as a way to make this fun) and more in the real-life parallels (the idea of private security firms having their employees purchase their own weapons and bullets&#8230; from the firm itself. &quot;Company store&quot; &#8212; but applied to the <em>military</em>).</p>
<p>Like, a world where we've coupled our military complex so deeply to corporate interests that corporations can sponsor drone-strikes (or where soldiers are charged <em>by the bullet</em> to maximize efficiency) is a thing that legit gives me anxiety. I might have gone a little too deep into the 'lol micro-transactions' end of this for that anxiety to really emerge, however.</p>

							</div>

								
					
